      "text": "### Lentinula edodes (Shiitake)"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-EDIBLE-FUNGI.md#015",
      "path": [
        "04-EDIBLE-FUNGI.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:mycology:04-edible-fungi, git-history:mycology:04-edible-fungi]",
        "canonical_path": "mycology/04-EDIBLE-FUNGI.md",
        "concepts": "[edible, fungi]",
        "current_path": "mycology/04-EDIBLE-FUNGI.md",
        "id": "maxim:mycology:edible-fungi",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "mycology",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[edible, fungi]",
        "section": "mycology",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Edible Fungi",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nSHIITAKE: second largest commercial mushroom globally\n  Native to East Asia; Japan dominant producer\n  Substrate: oak wood chips + wheat bran + water (log cultivation or bags)\n  Spawn: inoculate → colonize → fruiting after ~3 months (bags)\n         or 1–2 years (natural log cultivation)\n  Logs: traditional method; multiple years of production"
    },
